To dynamically display the frequency of words in a given text using HTML, we can use the following approach:
1. Create a textarea element in the HTML code where users can input the text to be analyzed.
2. Create a button element that, when clicked, will trigger a function to analyze the text.
3. Inside the function, we will retrieve the text from the textarea element and split it into an array of words.
4. We will then create an object to store the frequency of each word in the text, where the key is the word and the value is the number of times it appears in the text.
5. We will loop through the array of words and increment the count for each word in the object.
6. Finally, we will loop through the object and dynamically create HTML elements to display the word and its frequency.
Here is an example implementation of this approach:
```
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
	<title>Word Frequency Analyzer</title>
</head>
<body>
	<h1>Word Frequency Analyzer</h1>
	<label for="text">Enter text:</label>
	<textarea id="text"></textarea>
	<button onclick="analyze()">Analyze</button>
	<div id="results"></div>
	<script>
		function analyze() {
			// Retrieve text from textarea
			var text = document.getElementById("text").value;
			// Split text into array of words
			var words = text.split(/\s+/);
			// Create object to store word frequency
			var frequency = {};
			// Loop through array of words and increment frequency count
			for (var i = 0; i < words.length; i++) {
				var word = words[i].toLowerCase();
				if (frequency[word]) {
					frequency[word]++;
				} else {
					frequency[word] = 1;
				}
			}
			// Loop through frequency object and create HTML elements to display results
			var resultsDiv = document.getElementById("results");
			resultsDiv.innerHTML = "";
			for (var word in frequency) {
				var count = frequency[word];
				var p = document.createElement("p");
				p.innerHTML = word + ": " + count;
				resultsDiv.appendChild(p);
			}
		}
	</script>
</body>
</html>
```
To ensure accuracy in the analysis, we can convert all words to lowercase before storing them in the frequency object. This will ensure that words with different capitalization are counted as the same word. Additionally, we can use regular expressions to split the text into words, which will handle different types of whitespace and punctuation.

To ensure the Celsius to Fahrenheit data is accurate and up-to-date, it is important to regularly verify the data against reliable sources and update it as needed. This can be done by setting up automated processes to retrieve and update the data on a regular basis.
To ensure the security of the data, it should be stored in a secure database with proper access controls and encryption. It is also important to regularly monitor the database for any unauthorized access or suspicious activity. In the event of a data breach or security incident, a response plan should be in place to quickly address the issue and minimize any potential damage.
Here is an example SQL code that incorporates error handling and data validation to prevent issues with input and retrieval:
```
CREATE PROCEDURE ConvertCelsiusToFahrenheit
    @Celsius FLOAT,
    @Fahrenheit FLOAT OUTPUT
AS
BEGIN
    SET NOCOUNT ON;
    IF @Celsius < -273.15
    BEGIN
        RAISERROR('Invalid input: Celsius cannot be lower than -273.15', 16, 1);
        RETURN;
    END
    SET @Fahrenheit = @Celsius * 1.8 + 32;
    IF @Fahrenheit > 1000
    BEGIN
        RAISERROR('Invalid output: Fahrenheit cannot be higher than 1000', 16, 1);
        RETURN;
    END
END
```
