Can you provide an explanation of three principles of object-oriented programming using C++ code as an example?
Here are the three principles of object-oriented programming in C++:
1. Encapsulation: This principle states that data and methods that operate on that data should be encapsulated within a single entity, an object. This way, the data can be protected and manipulated only by the methods of that object. 
Example:
class Person {
    private:
        string name;
        int age;
    public:
        void setName(string n) {
            name = n;
        }
        void setAge(int a) {
            age = a;
        }
        string getName() {
            return name;
        }
        int getAge() {
            return age;
        }
};
2. Inheritance: This principle allows us to create a new class based on an existing class, inheriting all its attributes and methods. This helps us to reuse code and create a more specialized class.
Example:
class Animal {
    public:
        void eat() {
            cout << "The animal is eating" << endl;
        }
};
class Dog : public Animal {
    public:
        void bark() {
            cout << "The dog is barking" << endl;
        }
};
3. Polymorphism: This principle allows us to perform a single action in different ways. It is achieved through function overloading and virtual functions.
Example:
class Shape {
    public:
        virtual float area() {
            return 0;
        }
};
class Rectangle : public Shape {
    private:
        float length;
        float breadth;
    public:
        Rectangle(float l, float b) {
            length = l;
            breadth = b;
        }
        float area() {
            return length * breadth;
        }
};
class Circle : public Shape {
    private:
        float radius;
    public:
        Circle(float r) {
            radius = r;
        }
        float area() {
            return 3.14 * radius * radius;
        }
};

