To enhance the time complexity of O(log n) when determining the location of a hidden number in a sorted list, with the restriction of only being allowed to ask yes or no questions, we can use a modified binary search algorithm. 
Instead of simply asking if the middle element of the list is equal to the hidden number, we can ask a series of questions that would help us eliminate half of the remaining suspects in each iteration. 
For example, we can start by asking if the hidden number is greater than or less than the middle element of the list. If it is greater, we can eliminate the lower half of the list, and if it is less, we can eliminate the upper half. 
We can then repeat this process with the remaining half of the list until we have narrowed down the location of the hidden number to a single element. 
Here is an example set of questions we could ask:
1. Is the hidden number greater than or less than the middle element of the list?
2. Is the hidden number greater than or less than the middle element of the remaining half of the list?
3. Is the hidden number greater than or less than the middle element of the new remaining half of the list?
4. And so on, until the hidden number is found.
In C++, we can use the std::vector data type to represent the sorted list. Here is an example implementation of the modified binary search algorithm:
```
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
int findHiddenNumber(std::vector<int> list, int hiddenNumber) {
    int low = 0;
    int high = list.size() - 1;
    int mid;
    while (low <= high) {
        mid = (low + high) / 2;

        if (list[mid] == hiddenNumber) {
            return mid;
        }
        else if (list[mid] < hiddenNumber) {
            low = mid + 1;
        }
        else {
            high = mid - 1;
        }
    }
    return -1; // Hidden number not found
}
int main() {
    std::vector<int> list = {1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, 13, 15};
    int hiddenNumber = 9;
    int location = findHiddenNumber(list, hiddenNumber);
    if (location == -1) {
        std::cout << "Hidden number not found." << std::endl;
    }
    else {
        std::cout << "Hidden number found at index " << location << "." << std::endl;
    }
    return 0;
}
```
In this implementation, we start by setting the low and high indices to the beginning and end of the list, respectively. We then calculate the middle index and check if the middle element is equal to the hidden number. 
If it is not equal, we use the set of questions described above to eliminate half of the remaining suspects in each iteration. We continue this process until we have either found the hidden number or determined that it is not in the list. 
By asking a series of questions that eliminate half of the remaining suspects in each iteration, we can achieve a time complexity of O(log n) for finding the location of a hidden number in a sorted list, even with the restriction of only being allowed to ask yes or no questions.

