Here's a code snippet that demonstrates how to use a priority queue in C++ to efficiently pack your belongings into containers based on their size and importance:
```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <queue>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;
struct Item {
    int size;
    int importance;
};
struct CompareItems {
    bool operator()(const Item& a, const Item& b) {
        return a.importance < b.importance;
    }
};
int main() {
    int numItems, bagSize;
    cin >> numItems >> bagSize;
    priority_queue<Item, vector<Item>, CompareItems> pq;
    for (int i = 0; i < numItems; i++) {
        int size, importance;
        cin >> size >> importance;
        pq.push({size, importance});
    }
    int totalSize = 0;
    int numContainers = 0;
    while (!pq.empty() && totalSize < bagSize) {
        Item item = pq.top();
        pq.pop();
        if (totalSize + item.size <= bagSize) {
            totalSize += item.size;
            numContainers++;
        }
    }
    cout << numContainers << endl;
    return 0;
}
```
In this code, we define a `struct` called `Item` that represents each item we want to pack. Each `Item` has a `size` and an `importance`. We also define a `struct` called `CompareItems` that we'll use to compare `Item`s in the priority queue based on their importance.
In the `main` function, we read in the number of items we have and the size of our bags. We then create a priority queue of `Item`s and read in each item's size and importance, adding it to the priority queue.
We then loop through the priority queue, popping off the most important item each time. If we can fit the item into our bags without exceeding the bag size, we add the item's size to our total size and increment the number of containers we've used. We continue doing this until we run out of items or we fill up our bags.
Finally, we output the number of containers we used and return from the program.
